# Selectors — the single place reads are derived

## Context

`app/src/selectors.ts` exists so filtering/derivation logic (which tasks are
visible, how many remain) lives in one place instead of being copy-pasted
wherever state is read. Today it exports `visibleTasks(state)` and
`remainingCount(state)`. This rule attaches to **all** of `app/src` (not
just `selectors.ts`), because the violation it prevents — an inline
`state.tasks.filter(...)` — gets written at the call site, in some other
file.

## Rule

- New derived-data needs (a count, a filtered list, a lookup) get a new named
  selector function in `app/src/selectors.ts`, not an inline
  `state.tasks.filter(...)`/`.map(...)` written at the call site.
- Selectors are **pure functions** of `AppState` — no side effects, no
  reaching into `store` internals, no reading `Date.now()`/randomness inside a
  selector.
- Keep existing selector signatures (`visibleTasks(state: AppState): Task[]`,
  `remainingCount(state: AppState): number`) stable; add new exports rather
  than changing what these return.

## How to verify

1. `grep -rn "state.tasks.filter\|state.tasks.map" app/src` → matches only
   appear inside `app/src/selectors.ts` (and `app/src/reducer.ts`, which
   builds new state rather than deriving a read view).
2. Any new selector is a named export in `app/src/selectors.ts` with an
   `AppState` parameter and no side effects.
